    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The relationship between the Las Vegas shear zone and the Bitter Spring Valley fault in southeastern Nevada is poorly understood. Three possible geometric configurations are analyzed using the numerically simulated propagation paths of the faults and field observations at both local and regional scale. As an integral part of this analysis, a range of possible remote stress states which might have been operating in mid-Miocene time is also discussed.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
